\title[Transitivity partition functions of graded $G$-sets]
{Transitivity Partition Functions of Graded $G$-Sets:\\
Rationality, Exact Denominators, and a Finite-Difference Criterion}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $G$ be a group and let $Y=\bigsqcup_{n\ge 0}Y_n$ be a graded $G$-set: a sequence of
$G$-sets indexed by a grading parameter $n$. For each $r\ge 0$ define the $r$-th
transitivity count $t_r(Y_n)$ to be the number of $G$-orbits on injective $r$-tuples of
elements of $Y_n$. This integer equals $1$ precisely when $G$ acts $r$-transitively on
$Y_n$, and is bounded above by the descending factorial $|Y_n|^{\underline r}$ when $Y_n$
is finite; it is therefore an order parameter interpolating between total symmetry and
total rigidity. We study the transitivity partition function
$Z_r(q)=\sum_{n\ge 0}t_r(Y_n)q^n$ and prove that if the grades are eventually
$r$-transitive from an index $N$ on, then $Z_r$ is rational with denominator dividing
$(1-q)^{r+1}$ and numerator of degree at most $N+r$; analytically, for $|q|<1$,
$Z_r(q)=\sum_{n<N}t_r(Y_n)q^n+q^N/(1-q)$, so the only singularity is a simple pole at
$q=1$. The engine is a two-way finite-difference criterion: $(1-q)^s\sum_n a_nq^n$ is a
polynomial if and only if the $s$-th forward difference of $(a_n)$ eventually vanishes,
with the exact coefficient identity
$[q^{n+s}]\big((1-q)^s\sum_k a_kq^k\big)=(\Delta^s a)_n$. This equivalence yields
sharpness: the exponent $r+1$ is attained by the trivial action on grades of size $n$,
whose counts are $n^{\underline r}$, and intermediate pole orders occur, for instance the
integers translating $\ZZ/n\ZZ$, where $t_2=n-1$ gives denominator exactly $(1-q)^2$. A
descent theorem extends rationality to the total partition function
$\sum_n\big(\sum_{k\le r}t_k(Y_n)\big)q^n$, and a Burnside-type identity recasts all
coefficients as sums over the group of fixed-point counts.
\end{abstract}

